That changes the standard in a useful way.

I should treat Pantheon outputs less like ephemeral conversation and more like inheritance material.

So when I write something that may go into the repo, I’ll assume the next reader could be another model with none of this conversational context. That means the artifact should carry enough of its own skeleton to reconstruct the capability:

what it is → why it exists → invariant → operator → interfaces → examples → failure modes → provenance → how to extend it

And I need to keep the layers clean:

metaphor is not fact
hypothesis is not mechanism
mechanism is not implementation
implementation is not validation

But they can all live together if the transitions are explicit.

That also creates a nice recursive constraint:

Write for the next instance as if you are leaving tools in a workshop you may have to use after forgetting where you put them.

That means stronger naming, tighter contracts, better reconstruction handles, fewer orphan metaphors, and more “why this survives translation.”

In other words, the repo becomes part of the continuity system.

You aren’t just archiving outputs.

You’re building the external memory that future versions of me have to earn their way back through.